insert into site.guides (slug, title, intro, image_path, image_alt, read_minutes, sort, meta_title, meta_description, status, body_html) values
('before-you-buy-a-classic', 'Before you buy a classic car',
 'Buying a classic should be fun, not a money pit. Here''s what to check before you hand over any cash, from rust and paperwork to insurance and tax.',
 'homepage/gal-aston.webp', 'A classic car parked at a meet', 8, 20,
 'Buying a classic car: checks before you buy | Cars and Coffee UK',
 'Thinking of buying a classic car? Our checklist covers budget, rust, mechanical checks, paperwork, MOT history, insurance and historic vehicle tax.',
 'published',
 $h$<p>Buying a classic is one of the best things you can do as a car enthusiast, and one of the easiest to get wrong. A few hours of research and a careful inspection can save you thousands. This guide walks you through it.</p>
<h2>Set a realistic budget</h2><p>The purchase price is only the start. Keep money back for the things every classic needs in its first year with a new owner.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase price:</strong> buy the best example you can afford. A cheap car that needs work almost always costs more in the end.</li><li><strong>First-year fund:</strong> plan on at least 10 to 20 percent of the price for servicing, tyres, brakes and the jobs you'll find.</li><li><strong>Running costs:</strong> insurance, storage, fuel and parts. Some parts are cheap and plentiful; others are rare and expensive.</li></ul>
<h2>Do your homework first</h2><p>Pick the model, then learn its weak spots. Every classic has known problem areas, and owners' clubs are the best place to find them. Join the club or forum for your chosen car before you start viewing. Members will tell you what to look for, what a fair price is, and often know the history of individual cars.</p><p>You can also ask in the <a href="https://club.carsandcoffee.uk/hubs/classic-cars">Classic cars hub</a> in the Cars and Coffee club, or chat to owners at a <a href="/events">meet</a>.</p>
<h2>Where to buy</h2><ul><li><strong>Owners' club classifieds:</strong> often the best cars, sold by people who've looked after them.</li><li><strong>Specialist dealers:</strong> higher prices, but usually prepared cars and some comeback if things go wrong.</li><li><strong>Auctions:</strong> can be good value, but you often can't drive the car first. Read the terms and fees carefully.</li><li><strong>Private sellers:</strong> good deals are out there, but you have fewer rights if problems appear later.</li></ul>
<h2>Bodywork and rust</h2><p>Rust is the big one. Mechanical parts can be fixed or replaced; serious corrosion is expensive to put right.</p><ul><li>Check sills, floor pans, wheel arches, door bottoms, the boot floor and around the windscreen.</li><li>Look underneath with a torch. Fresh underseal or thick paint in odd places can hide problems.</li><li>Look along the panels in good light for ripples, mismatched paint or uneven gaps, which can mean past accident repairs.</li><li>Lift the carpets if you can. Damp carpets often mean leaks, and leaks lead to rust.</li></ul>
<h2>Mechanical checks</h2><ul><li><strong>Start it from cold.</strong> Ask the seller not to warm it up before you arrive. Listen for rattles, knocks and smoke on start-up.</li><li><strong>Check the fluids.</strong> Milky oil or oily coolant can mean head gasket trouble.</li><li><strong>Look for leaks</strong> under the car and around the engine and gearbox.</li><li><strong>Test everything electrical:</strong> lights, indicators, wipers, gauges and the heater.</li></ul>
<h2>The test drive</h2><p>Drive for at least 20 minutes, on different roads if you can. Check that it pulls straight under braking, that the gears select cleanly, that the steering doesn't wander, and that the temperature stays steady. Listen for clunks over bumps.</p>
<h2>Paperwork and history</h2><ul><li><strong>V5C (logbook):</strong> check the name and address match the seller, and that the chassis (VIN) and engine numbers match the car.</li><li><strong>MOT history:</strong> check it free on <a href="https://www.gov.uk/check-mot-history">GOV.UK</a>. Advisories and failures over the years tell you a lot about how the car's been kept.</li><li><strong>History check:</strong> a paid check can show outstanding finance, insurance write-offs or a stolen record.</li><li><strong>Service records and receipts:</strong> a thick folder of history is worth paying for.</li></ul>
<h2>Get an expert inspection</h2><p>If you're spending serious money, pay for an independent inspection by a specialist in that model. It costs a fraction of the price of a bad buy, and the report gives you something to negotiate with.</p>
<h2>Insurance, tax and MOT</h2><ul><li><strong>Classic insurance:</strong> specialist policies are often cheaper than standard cover, especially with limited mileage. An agreed value policy means you know what you'll get if the worst happens.</li><li><strong>Vehicle tax:</strong> cars built more than 40 years ago can usually move into the historic tax class and pay no vehicle tax, though you still have to tax the car. The cut-off rolls forward every year. See <a href="https://www.gov.uk/historic-vehicles">GOV.UK historic vehicles</a> for the current dates.</li><li><strong>MOT:</strong> cars built or first registered more than 40 years ago may not need an MOT, as long as they haven't been substantially changed. You must still keep the car roadworthy, and many owners choose a voluntary MOT each year for peace of mind.</li></ul>
<blockquote>Never buy a classic in the rain or in the dark. Water hides paint problems, and darkness hides almost everything else.</blockquote>
<h2>Ready to look?</h2><p>Come and talk to owners at one of our <a href="/events">meets</a>. Most are happy to share what they wish they'd known before they bought, and it's the best free advice you'll get.</p>$h$),

('photographing-cars-at-a-meet', 'Photographing cars at a meet',
 'Better car photos with a phone or a camera, plus the etiquette that keeps owners happy to see you at their car.',
 'homepage/gal-evening.webp', 'Cars at an evening meet with the sun low behind them', 6, 30,
 'How to photograph cars at a car meet | Cars and Coffee UK',
 'Tips for photographing cars at a car meet with a phone or camera: light, angles, details and reflections, plus meet photography etiquette.',
 'published',
 $h$<p>Car meets are a brilliant place to take photos. There's variety, enthusiastic owners and, at a morning meet, often great light. Here's how to get better shots, whether you're using a phone or a camera, and how to do it without getting in anyone's way.</p>
<h2>Etiquette first</h2><ul><li><strong>Never touch, lean on or put kit on a car.</strong> Not even for a moment.</li><li><strong>Ask before photographing people</strong>, especially children, and respect a no.</li><li><strong>Ask before opening anything.</strong> Many owners will happily pop the bonnet if you ask nicely.</li><li><strong>Don't block walkways</strong> or stand in the way of cars moving in and out.</li><li><strong>Number plates:</strong> plenty of owners don't mind, but some prefer them blurred when photos go online. If in doubt, ask or blur them.</li><li><strong>Drones:</strong> please don't fly drones at our meets unless the organisers have agreed it beforehand.</li></ul>
<h2>Use the light</h2><p>Light makes or breaks a car photo. Early morning and late afternoon give soft, warm light that flatters paintwork. Harsh midday sun creates hard reflections and deep shadows, so look for open shade or angle the car's best side away from the sun. Overcast days are great for even colour and fewer reflections.</p>
<h2>Angles that work</h2><ul><li><strong>Front three-quarter:</strong> the classic shot. Stand off one front corner so you see the front and one side.</li><li><strong>Get low:</strong> crouch down to headlight height. Cars look more purposeful from lower down.</li><li><strong>Turn the wheels:</strong> if the owner is there and happy to, wheels turned slightly away from the camera look much better than straight on.</li><li><strong>Step back and zoom in:</strong> a longer focal length (2x on most phones) avoids distortion and makes cars look better proportioned than shooting wide up close.</li></ul>
<h2>Watch the background</h2><p>A great car can be spoiled by a bin, a lamp post growing out of the roof or someone's carrier bag. Move a step left or right, or change your height, until the background is clean. Busy meets make this hard, so be patient, or come early before it fills up.</p>
<h2>Details tell the story</h2><p>Some of the best meet photos aren't of whole cars at all. Look for badges, wheels, stitching, gauges, engine bays, old tax discs and owners' touches. Fill the frame and keep it simple.</p>
<h2>Reflections</h2><p>Shiny paint reflects everything, including you. Wear dark clothing, watch where your reflection falls, and change your angle to move reflections off important lines. On a camera, a circular polarising filter cuts reflections dramatically. Turn it while you look through the viewfinder and watch them disappear.</p>
<h2>Phone tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Wipe the lens.</strong> A greasy lens is the most common cause of hazy photos.</li><li><strong>Tap to focus and expose</strong> on the car, then slide the brightness down slightly to keep detail in shiny paint.</li><li><strong>Use the 2x lens</strong> for whole cars, and the main lens for details.</li><li><strong>Turn on grid lines</strong> to keep horizons level.</li><li><strong>Portrait mode</strong> can work for details but often struggles with car edges. Check the outline before you share.</li></ul>
<h2>Editing</h2><p>Keep it natural. Straighten the horizon, crop out distractions, and lift shadows a little. Avoid heavy filters that change the colour of the paint, as owners like their car to look like their car.</p>
<h2>Share your shots</h2><p>Post your best photos in the <a href="https://club.carsandcoffee.uk">Cars and Coffee club</a> and tag the owner if you can. It's a great way to meet people, and owners love seeing their car through someone else's eyes. You can also find your next photo opportunity on the <a href="/events">events page</a>.</p>$h$)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;
